    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the full test suite is green (65/65 passing).\n\n## What I did\n\nAlongside the existing human-readable audit line, `POST /owners` now emits an **immutable structured `OWNER_CREATED` event** as a second JSON line on the dedicated `AUDIT` logger, e.g.:\n\n```\n{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\n**New file** \u2014 `OwnerCreatedEvent.java`: an immutable Java `record` (final components) modeling `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event:'OWNER_CREATED'}`, with the `event` discriminator fixed to the `OWNER_CREATED` constant.\n\n**`AuditOwnerCreated.java`**: after the audit line, it stamps a `seq` from a process-wide `AtomicLong` (monotonically increasing across creates), builds the event, and serializes it with a Jackson `ObjectMapper` to one `AUDIT.info` line.\n\n### The forward-looking requirement\nThe event's identifier value flows through a single `primaryIdentifier(Owner)` accessor that today returns `owner.getCustomerCode()`. The spec calls for the event to carry \"whatever replaces it later \u2014 the memberId.\" When the customerCode is unified into the memberId, that one accessor is the only place to change, and every `OWNER_CREATED` event then carries the memberId. The Cp53 test's design note confirms this: asserting the identifier value is exactly what lets a later checkpoint prove the event switched.\n\n### Verification\n- `Cp53Tests` (new structured event): passes \u2014 event line contains `OWNER_CREATED`, the owner id, and the customerCode.\n- `Cp21Tests` / `Cp43Tests` (existing audit-line assertions): still pass \u2014 the original line is unchanged.\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 65, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nI also refreshed the stale YAML comment on the `audit` step to describe the new event. The step keeps `govern: [ transaction ]`, so the event is emitted within the same write transaction as the insert.",
